<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-logarithms" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Logarithms</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this section, we learn how to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ol>
			<li><p>Evaluate logarithms.</p></li>
			<li><p>Apply the definition of a logarithm to solve equations.</p></li>
    </ol>
	</objectives>

  <subsection>
    <title>Definition of logarithm</title>

    <p>
      We discuss the exponential function <m>f(x) = b^x</m> for a fixed base <m>b &gt; 0</m> and <m>b \neq 1</m>.
      In fact, for now, we only focus on the case where <m>b &gt; 1</m>.
      Recall from <xref ref="principle-exponential-function-properties" /> that <m>f(x) = b^x</m> is a one-to-one function.
      (We can see this because the graph of <m>f(x) = b^x</m> passes the Horizontal Line Test, 
      so by <xref ref="horizontal-line-test-iff-one-to-one"/>, <m>f(x) = b^x</m> is one-to-one.)
    </p>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="principle-every-one-to-one-has-inverse"/>, we saw that every one-to-one function has an inverse function.
      So, <m>f(x) = b^x</m> has an inverse function: <m>f^{-1}(x) = \log_b(x)</m>.
      Note that <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> is the standard notation for the inverse of a function <m>f(x)</m>,
      but because this inverse function of <m>b^x</m> will come up often, it is given the special notation <m>\log_b(x)</m>.
    </p>
    <note>
      <p>
        The function <m>f^{-1}(x) = \log_b(x)</m> is called the <em>logarithmic function</em> with base <m>b</m>.
        The logarithmic function is the inverse of the exponential function.
        We read <m>\log_b(x)</m> as <q>log base <m>b</m> of <m>x</m></q>.
        It is <em>important</em> to say the word <q>base</q> before saying <m>b</m>, and it is also important to say the word <q>of</q> to remind us that we are talking about a function: in particular, there is no multiplication going on here.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the past, we have only been accustomed to a function name being a single letter, like <m>f</m> or <m>g</m>, and then we wrote <m>f(x)</m> or <m>g(x)</m> to denote the function value at <m>x</m>.
        Now, we have <m>\log_b(x)</m> to denote the value of this function, and the function name is <m>\log_b</m>, so consists of more than a single letter: 
        it is the word <q>log</q> with a subscript <m>b</m> representing a specific number.
      </p>
    </note>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\log_2(x)</m> is a read as <q>log base <m>2</m> of <m>x</m></q>, and is an example of a logarithm function.
            The inverse of the exponential function <m>f(x) = 2^x</m> is the logarithmic function <m>f^{-1}(x) = \log_2(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\log_3(x)</m> is a read as <q>log base <m>3</m> of <m>x</m></q>, and is an example of a logarithm function.
            The inverse of the exponential function <m>f(x) = 3^x</m> is the logarithmic function <m>f^{-1}(x) = \log_3(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      The fact that <m>f(x) = b^x</m> has as its inverse function <m>f^{-1}(x) = \log_b(x)</m> means that 
      we can start from the graph of <m>f(x) = b^x</m> and reflect it across the line <m>y=x</m> to get the graph of <m>f^{-1}(x) = \log_b(x)</m>.
      Thus, from the generic graph of an exponential function <m>f(x) = b^x</m>
      <figure>
        <image width="65%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, blue, domain=-5.:3.37,smooth,samples=100] plot (\x,{1.7^\x});
                \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <caption>Graph of <m>y=b^x</m></caption>
      </figure>          
      we flip the graph diagonally to obtain the graph of <m>f^{-1}(x) = \log_b(x)</m>
      <figure>
        <image width="65%">
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \draw[<->] (-6,0) -- (6,0); 
                \node at (6,.4) {$x$};
                \draw[<->] (0,-6) -- (0,6);
                \node at (.4,6) {$y$};
                \draw[<->, ultra thick, red, domain=-5:3.37,smooth,samples=100] plot ({1.7^\x},\x);
                \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <caption>Graph of <m>y=\log_b(x)</m></caption>
      </figure>
      Because <m>y=b^x</m> has the point <m>(0,1)</m> as a <m>y</m>-intercept, the graph of <m>y=\log_b(x)</m> has the point <m>(1,0)</m> as an <m>x</m>-intercept.
      In <xref ref="principle-inverse-domain-range"/>, we saw that the roles of the domain and range change places for inverse functions.
      Since the domain of <m>f(x) = b^x</m> is <m>(-\infty, \infty)</m>, the range of <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> is <m>(-\infty, \infty)</m>.
      Since the range of <m>f(x) = b^x</m> is <m>(0, \infty)</m>, the domain of <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> is <m>(0, \infty)</m>.
      Since <m>f(x) = b^x</m> has the horizontal line <m>y=0</m> as a horizontal asymptote, the graph of <m>f^{-1}(x) = \log_b(x)</m> has the vertical line <m>x=0</m> as a vertical asymptote.
      <principle xml:id="principle-log-function-facts">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The function <m>g(x) = \log_b(x)</m>:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  has domain <m>(0,\infty)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  has range <m>(-\infty,\infty)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  has the point <m>(1,0)</m> as an <m>x</m>-intercept
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  has the vertical line <m>x=0</m> as a vertical asymptote
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
    </p>
    <p>
      Compare: square root
    </p>

    <p>
      The fact that <m>f(x) = b^x</m> has as its inverse function <m>f^{-1}(x) = \log_b(x)</m> means that the following:
      <m>b^r = s</m> if and only if <m>\log_b(s) = r</m>.
      This holds because of <xref ref="principle-one-to-one-points-function"/>.
      What this means is that if we have an equation of the form <m>b^r = s</m>, we can rewrite it in logarithmic form as <m>\log_b(s) = r</m>,
      and when we have an equation of the form <m>\log_b(s) = r</m>, we can rewrite it in exponential form as <m>b^r = s</m>.
      It is helpful to see this definition of logarithm in a visual way, which we call the Circle Trick:     
      <principle xml:id="principle-circle-trick">
        <title>Circle Trick: the definition of logarithm</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <figure>
              <image width="45%">
                <latex-image>
                  <![CDATA[
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \node at (0,0) {$b^r = s$ becomes $\log_b(s) = r$};
                    % Exp equation
                    \draw [->, thick, blue] (-1.3,0.2) [out=90,in=90] to (-1.95,0.22);
                    \draw [->, thick, blue] (-1.95,-0.1) [out=270,in=270] to (-1.35,-0.2);
                    % Log equation
                    \draw [->, thick, blue] (2,0.2) [out=90,in=90] to (1.25,0.2);
                    \draw [->, thick, blue] (1.3,-0.2) [out=270,in=270] to (2,-0.2);
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                  ]]>
                </latex-image>
              </image>
              <caption>Circle Trick: a visual representation of logarithms and exponential functions</caption>
            </figure>            
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      The thing to note is that the base <m>b</m> in one equation remains the base <m>b</m> in the other equation.
      The arrows drawn in have the general shape of a circle, and so we call this the Circle Trick.
      In the two equations, the <m>r</m> and <m>s</m> switch places, and the base <m>b</m> remains the same.
    </p>    

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Apply the Circle Trick to rewrite <m>c^m = n</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_c(n) = m</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Apply the Circle Trick to rewrite <m>2^t = u</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_2(u) = t</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>


    <p>
      <example xml:id="solve-using-circle-trick">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Apply the Circle Trick to rewrite <m>2^x = 9</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_2(9) = x</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      The last example shows us that we were able to solve for <m>x</m>.
      Note that taking <m>2^x=9</m> and dividing both sides by <m>2</m> does not help.
    </p>


    <p>
      <example xml:id="log-base-and-argument-warmup1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Apply the Circle Trick to rewrite <m>3^x = 9</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_3(9) = x</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      While we applied the Circle Trick to rewrite <m>3^x = 9</m> as <m>\log_3(9) = x</m>, we can also solve for <m>x</m> by recognizing that <m>9 = 3^2</m>, so <m>x=2</m>.
      This shows us that <m>\log_3(9) = 2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <example xml:id="log-base-and-argument-warmup2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Apply the Circle Trick to rewrite <m>2^x = 16</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_2(16) = x</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
      While we applied the Circle Trick to rewrite <m>2^x = 16</m> as <m>\log_2(16) = x</m>, we can also solve for <m>x</m> by recognizing that <m>16 = 2^4</m>, so <m>x=4</m>.
      This shows us that <m>\log_2(16) = 4</m>.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      The comments after <xref ref="log-base-and-argument-warmup1"/> and <xref ref="log-base-and-argument-warmup2"/> 
      show us that, based on the Circle Trick, when we are given an expression of the format <m>\log_b(a)</m> we can simplify this expression by recognizing that <m>a = b^c</m> for some value of <m>c</m>.
      In other words, <m>\log_b(a)</m> simplifies to the value of the exponent when we ask the question: <m>b</m> to the <em>what power</em> equals <m>a</m>?
      <principle xml:id="principle-log-base-and-argument-thinking">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If we are asked to simplify <m>\log_b(a)</m>, and <m>b</m> and <m>a</m> are both given constants (not variables), then the given expression simplifies
            to the blank in the question: <q><m>b</m> to the <m>\underline{\phantom{....}}</m> equals <m>a</m>?</q>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_2(8)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_2(8) = 3</m>, because <m>2^3 = 8</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_3(81)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_3(81) = 4</m>, because <m>3^4 = 81</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      We will look at more examples in a moment, but we make the analogy to radicals:
      The question <m>\sqrt[2]{9}</m> has answer <m>3</m> because <m>3^2 = 9</m>.
      In the case of a radical, the expression simplifies to the value of the missing base.
      The question <m>\log_2(8)</m> has answer <m>3</m> because <m>2^3 = 8</m>.
      In the case of a logarithm, the expression simplifies to the value of the missing exponent.
    </p>


    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_5(125)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_5(125) = 3</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_7(49)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_7(49) = 2</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_7(1)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_7(1) = 0</m> because <m>7^0=1</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_8(1)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_8(1) = 0</m> because <m>8^0=1</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      The last two examples show us that for any base <m>b</m>, we have <m>\log_b(1) = 0</m> because <m>b^0 = 1</m> from <xref ref="principle-exponent-rules-4"/>.      
      <principle xml:id="principle-log-of-1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\log_b(1) = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      This actually repackages the information from <xref ref="principle-log-function-facts"/> the graph of <m>g(x) = \log_b(x)</m> goes through the point <m>(1,0)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now, what happens if the input to a logarithm is a negative number or zero?
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_9(-1)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_9(-1)</m> is undefined because the equation <m>9^x=-1</m> has no solution.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_{10}(-2)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_{10}(-2)</m> is undefined because the equation <m>10^x=-2</m> has no solution.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\log_{11}(0)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\log_{11}(0)</m> is undefined because the equation <m>11^x=0</m> has no solution.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
      The last three examples show that for any base <m>b</m>, the expression <m>\log_b(x)</m> is undefined for any negative value of <m>x</m> because the equation <m>b^x = y</m> has no solution for any negative value of <m>y</m>.
      <principle xml:id="principle-log-of-zero-or-negative">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>x \leq 0</m>, then <m>\log_b(x)</m> is undefined.
            In other words, it is only valid to take the logarithm (in anyy base) of a positive number.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      This actually repackages the information from <xref ref="principle-log-function-facts"/> that the domain of <m>g(x) = \log_b(x)</m> is <m>(0,\infty)</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>

	<subsection>
		<title>Special bases</title>

    <p>
      Two numbers appear so often as bases of logarithms that they have special names and special notation.
    </p>
    <principle>
      <p>
        <m>\ln(z) = \log_e(z)</m>
      </p>
    </principle>
    <p>
      When we write <m>\ln(z)</m> we can read it as <q>natural log of <m>z</m></q>.
    </p>
    <principle>
      <p>
        <m>\log(z) = \log_{10}(z)</m>
      </p>
    </principle>
    <p>
      Note that both are just special cases of logarithms.
      For example, <m>\ln(5)</m> is just shorthand notation for <m>\log_e(5)</m>, and <m>\log(5)</m> is just shorthand notation for <m>\log_{10}(5)</m>.
      In particular, we earlier mentioned that <m>\log_b(1)=0</m> for any base <m>b</m>, so we have <m>\ln(1) = 0</m> and <m>\log(1) = 0</m>.
      These are not really two new facts, but it's just putting the information thot <m>\log_e(1)=0</m> and <m>\log_{10}(1)=0</m> into the special notation.
    </p>

    <p>
      On paper, it is perfectly acceptable if the final answer to a question includes an expression such as <m>\log_2(9)</m>.
      This is because <m>\log_2(9)</m> is a number, and specifically, it is the answer to the question <m>2</m> to the <em>what power</em> equals <m>9</m>?
      It is not a particularly nice number (some decimal number), but it is a number.
    </p>
    <p>
      As fine as it is for <m>\log_2(9)</m> to be the final answer (or part of a final answer) on paper, for practical applications,
      we often want to know the decimal approximation of <m>\log_2(9)</m>.
      However, we run into the following problem:
      How could a calculator have a button for a <q>log base 2</q>, and a button for a <q>log base 3</q>, and a button for a <q>log base 4</q>, and so on?
      A calculator would run out of room.
      Fortunately, we have the following formula which will help us rewrite final answers:
      <principle xml:id="principle-change-of-base-general">
        <title>Change of Base Formula</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\log_b(c) = \dfrac{\log_a(c)}{\log_a(b)}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
      Notice that <m>a</m> does not appear on the left side at all, and appears twice on the right side.
      So, when using this formula to turn an expression of the form on the left into the form on the right, we actually get to choose the base <m>a</m> on the right side ourselves.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, suppose we had the expression <m>\log_2(9)</m>. Here are several ways to rewrite this expression using the change of base formula:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\log_2(9) = \dfrac{\log_{7}(9)}{\log_{7}(2)}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\log_2(9) = \dfrac{\log_{8}(9)}{\log_{8}(2)}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\log_2(9) = \dfrac{\log_{301}(9)}{\log_{301}(2)}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\log_2(9) = \dfrac{\log_{e}(9)}{\log_{e}(2)} = \dfrac{\ln(9)}{\ln(2)}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      In fact, the last example shows that if we wish, we can always choose to rewrite a logarithm expression in terms of a natural logarithm.
      Here is a special case of the Change of Base Formula:
      <principle xml:id="principle-change-of-base-special">
        <title>Change of Base Formula: special case</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Any logarithm expression can be rewritten as the quotient of two outpus of the natural logarithm function.
          </p>
          <p>
            <m>\log_b(c) = \dfrac{\ln(c)}{\ln(b)}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </principle>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\log_5(6) = \dfrac{\ln(6)}{\ln(5)}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
    </p>
    <p>
    <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\log_7(8) = \dfrac{\ln(8)}{\ln(7)}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
    </p>

  </subsection>

</section>